APX.uy = {"country":{"name":"Uruguay","tagline":"An Atlantic-cooled pocket between the River Plate and the ocean, where a Basque pioneer's Tannat became a national identity.","classification":"Uruguay has no formal denomination of origin system. Wine law, administered by INAVI under laws 15.903 (1987) and 18.462 (2009), divides wine into two main categories: *Vino de Calidad Preferente* (VCP), quality wine from Vitis vinifera, bottled in containers of 75 cl or less, with registered labels; and *Vino Común* (VC), table wine often sold in demijohns or cartons. Under MERCOSUR rules a varietal or vintage claim requires at least 85% of the named grape or year.\n\nGeographic names on labels follow the country's 19 administrative departments (Canelones, Maldonado, Colonia and so on), and producers may use a finer place name if all grapes come from it. The industry and INAVI's promotion group departments into informal regions, commonly six: Metropolitana (Canelones, Montevideo, San José), Oceánica (Maldonado, Rocha, Lavalleja), Litoral Sur (Colonia, Soriano), Litoral Norte (Salto, Paysandú, Artigas), Centro (Durazno, Florida, Tacuarembó) and Norte (Rivera). These groupings have no legal force, and Uruguay notified only the national name for protection in the Mercosur GI agreement. Uruguay was the first South American country to ban foreign place names such as 'Champagne' on domestic labels, in 1993."},"regions":[{"name":"Metropolitana","slug":"metropolitana","tagline":"Canelones, Montevideo and San José: the clay hills where Uruguayan Tannat was built.","ids":["uy-canelones","uy-montevideo","uy-san-jose"]},{"name":"Oceánica","slug":"oce-nica","tagline":"Maldonado's granite hills by the Atlantic.","ids":["uy-lavalleja","uy-maldonado","uy-rocha"]},{"name":"Litoral Sur","slug":"litoral-sur","tagline":"Colonia and the River Plate shore.","ids":["uy-colonia","uy-soriano"]},{"name":"Litoral Norte","slug":"litoral-norte","tagline":"Salto, where Harriague's Tannat began.","ids":["uy-artigas","uy-paysandu","uy-rio-negro","uy-salto"]},{"name":"Centro","slug":"centro","tagline":"The interior: experimentation in Durazno.","ids":["uy-durazno","uy-flores","uy-florida","uy-tacuarembo","uy-treinta-y-tres"]},{"name":"Norte","slug":"norte","tagline":"Rivera's red sands on the Brazilian border.","ids":["uy-cerro-largo","uy-rivera"]}],"items":{"uy-artigas":{"name":"Artigas","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Litoral Norte","area_ha":115,"styles":["red","white"],"summary":"Artigas, the northernmost department, grows grapes around Bella Unión on the Brazilian border, in Uruguay's warmest climate (about 115 ha in 2013).","classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"litoral-norte"},"uy-canelones":{"name":"Canelones","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Metropolitana","area_ha":5046,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Tannat, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Marselan, Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay, Albariño, Muscat Hamburg","summary":"Canelones, immediately north of Montevideo, is Uruguay's wine heartland: by most accounts more than 60% of national production, with Montevideo together over three-quarters of the vineyard. Its gently rolling hills of heavy clay and clay-calcareous soils (around Las Piedras, Progreso, Juanicó, Canelón Chico, Las Violetas and Melilla's edge) are home to the historic family wineries of Italian, Basque and Spanish descent: Pisano, Pizzorno, Carrau, Stagnari, Juanicó, Marichal, Toscanini, De Lucca, Varela Zarranz. Tannat is king, making structured, ageable reds whose tannins are tamed by the maritime humidity, alongside Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Marselan, Sauvignon Blanc and a growing range of Italian and Spanish varieties. INAVI itself is headquartered in Las Piedras.","terroir":"Rolling hills of heavy clay and clay-calcareous soils; humid maritime climate with about 1,000 mm of rain; sea breezes from the River Plate.","producers":["Pisano","Pizzorno","Bodegas Carrau","Establecimiento Juanicó (Familia Deicas)","Marichal","Stagnari","De Lucca","Varela Zarranz","Viñedo de los Vientos"],"nerd_note":"Uruguay grows nearly a third of the world's Tannat, by INAVI and University of Adelaide estimates, and most of it is in Canelones.","classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"metropolitana"},"uy-cerro-largo":{"name":"Cerro Largo","cat":"Departamento","subcat":"Department","region":"Norte","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Tannat","summary":"Cerro Largo, in north-eastern Uruguay on the Brazilian border around Melo, is cattle and rice country with very little commercial viticulture. Its rolling grasslands continue into Brazil's Campanha Gaúcha, where vineyards have spread since the 2000s on similar soils and climate, so it is a plausible future frontier for Tannat.","terroir":"Rolling grassland on sandstone and basalt-derived soils, warmer and more continental than the southern coast.","nerd_note":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"norte"},"uy-colonia":{"name":"Colonia","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Litoral Sur","area_ha":563,"styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"summary":"Colonia, on the River Plate opposite Buenos Aires, has the oldest commercial winery in Uruguay, Los Cerros de San Juan (1854), and a hub of wineries around Carmelo, including Irurtia, Narbona and the Zubizarreta family. The warmer river influence and sedimentary soils make Tannat fuller and riper here.","producers":["Los Cerros de San Juan","Irurtia","Narbona","El Legado"],"classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"litoral-sur"},"uy-durazno":{"name":"Durazno","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Centro","area_ha":89,"styles":["red","white"],"summary":"Durazno, in the centre of the country, has a small, experimental wine scene led by Bodega El Capricho, an early planter of Verdejo and Mencía in Uruguay. Supple reds from warmer inland conditions.","classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"centro"},"uy-flores":{"name":"Flores","cat":"Departamento","subcat":"Department","region":"Centro","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Tannat","summary":"Flores, Uruguay's least populous department, around Trinidad in the centre of the country, has had no registered vineyards since the late 2000s according to official statistics, after earlier small plantings were abandoned.","terroir":"Basalt and sedimentary plains.","nerd_note":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"centro"},"uy-florida":{"name":"Florida","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Centro","area_ha":49,"styles":["red"],"summary":"Florida department, north of Canelones, has a few dozen hectares of vines (about 50 ha in 2013).","classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"centro"},"uy-lavalleja":{"name":"Lavalleja","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Oceánica","styles":["red","white"],"summary":"Lavalleja, inland from Maldonado in the eastern sierras, has few vineyards, but Familia Deicas farms an extreme site on the volcanic outcrop of Cerro del Guazuvirá, a rare volcanic terroir in Uruguay.","producers":["Familia Deicas"],"classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"oce-nica"},"uy-maldonado":{"name":"Maldonado","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Oceánica","area_ha":252,"styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Albariño, Tannat, Pinot Noir, Cabernet Franc, Sauvignon Blanc, Marselan","summary":"Maldonado is Uruguay's fastest-growing and most glamorous wine region, on granite hills between Punta del Este, José Ignacio and the sierras inland. Nobody planted here until Alto de la Ballena in 2001, because the rocky, poor soils gave low yields; that is exactly what attracted the quality pioneers. The ocean is close, winds are strong and summers fresher, so Tannat comes out brighter and Albariño, Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc and Cabernet Franc shine.\n\nBodega Garzón, built near Pueblo Garzón by Argentine billionaire Alejandro Bulgheroni and opened in 2016, put the department on the world map with hundreds of small vineyard blocks on granite and ballast-like decomposed rock. Viña Edén, Bodega Oceánica José Ignacio, Familia Deicas's Atlántico Sur and Cerro del Toro are among the others.","terroir":"Granite, gneiss and decomposed 'balasto' rock on hills up to a few hundred metres; strong Atlantic winds; cooler summers.","producers":["Bodega Garzón","Alto de la Ballena","Viña Edén","Bodega Oceánica José Ignacio","Cerro del Toro","Familia Deicas (Atlántico Sur)"],"nerd_note":"Garzón's vineyard is split into a large number of small blocks to follow the patchwork of granite and soil depth.","classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"oce-nica"},"uy-montevideo":{"name":"Montevideo","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Metropolitana","area_ha":1073,"styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"summary":"Montevideo department holds the historic suburban vineyards of the capital, at Melilla, Colón, Peñarol and Carrasco. Francisco Vidiella made the first harvest of European vines at Colón in 1883, and Bodega Bouza, Carrau's Colón winery, Bodega Santa Rosa and others keep the tradition alive within city limits. Conditions mirror Canelones: clay soils, river-moderated climate, Tannat, Merlot and Albariño.","producers":["Bodega Bouza","Bodegas Carrau (Colón)","Bodega Santa Rosa","Bodega Cerro Chapeu"],"classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"metropolitana"},"uy-paysandu":{"name":"Paysandú","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Litoral Norte","area_ha":172,"styles":["red"],"summary":"Paysandú, on the Uruguay river, has about 170 ha of vines (2013) on sedimentary soils in a warm, continental climate; Tannat and Cabernet ripen fully here.","classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"litoral-norte"},"uy-rio-negro":{"name":"Río Negro","cat":"Departamento","subcat":"Department","region":"Litoral Norte","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Tannat","summary":"Río Negro department lies on the Uruguay river around Fray Bentos and Young. Its small vineyards disappeared between roughly 1989 and 2007 according to official vineyard statistics, so today it is a department with a wine past rather than a current scene.","terroir":"Alluvial and sedimentary soils near the river, with hot summers.","nerd_note":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"litoral-norte"},"uy-rivera":{"name":"Rivera","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Norte","area_ha":47,"styles":["red","white"],"summary":"Rivera, on the Brazilian border, is Uruguay's most distinctive northern terroir: deep, red, sandy soils derived from sandstone, very free-draining in a warmer, sunnier climate. The Carrau family pioneered here from the 1970s with the Cerro Chapeu vineyard; the wines, notably Tannat and Cabernet, are fragrant and juicy with silky tannins.","producers":["Bodegas Carrau (Cerro Chapeu)","Cerros de Oro"],"classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"norte"},"uy-rocha":{"name":"Rocha","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Oceánica","styles":["white","red"],"summary":"Rocha, the easternmost coastal department towards the Brazilian border, is Uruguay's newest wine frontier, with a handful of small vineyards in windswept coastal and sierra sites.","classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"oce-nica"},"uy-salto":{"name":"Salto","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Litoral Norte","area_ha":58,"styles":["red"],"summary":"Salto, on the Uruguay river in the north-west, is where Pascual Harriague planted Tannat around 1870 and where the grape's Uruguayan story began. Today its vineyards are small (about 60 ha in 2013), in a warmer, more continental climate.","nerd_note":"Uruguayans called Tannat 'Harriague' in the founder's honour for decades.","classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"litoral-norte"},"uy-san-jose":{"name":"San José","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Metropolitana","area_ha":502,"styles":["red","white"],"summary":"San José, west of Canelones, is pastoral cattle country with a significant vineyard (about 500 ha in 2013) on the gentle hills around Libertad, Ecilda Paullier and Rincón del Pino. Conditions are similar to Canelones, slightly more influenced by the River Plate.","classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"metropolitana"},"uy-soriano":{"name":"Soriano","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Litoral Sur","styles":["red"],"summary":"Soriano, on the Uruguay river north of Colonia, has a small vineyard area among the grain farms around Dolores and Mercedes.","classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"litoral-sur"},"uy-tacuarembo":{"name":"Tacuarembó","cat":"Department","subcat":"Department","region":"Centro","area_ha":28,"styles":["red"],"summary":"Tacuarembó, in the north-central interior, has small emerging plantings (about 30 ha in 2013).","classification":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"centro"},"uy-treinta-y-tres":{"name":"Treinta y Tres","cat":"Departamento","subcat":"Department","region":"Centro","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Tannat","summary":"Treinta y Tres, in the east of Uruguay, takes its name from the 'Thirty-Three Orientals' who launched the 1825 independence campaign. Official statistics record no remaining vineyards here since about 2007, after small plantings faded away.","terroir":"Granitic and sandy soils in hilly country.","nerd_note":"Uruguay has no legal appellations; geographic indications on labels follow the 19 departments.","rslug":"centro"}}};
